<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">United States Army Military Government in Korea</h1><p class="st-meta">1945 CE – 1948 CE · capital Seoul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The American military government that ruled Korea south of the 38th parallel from 1945 until the founding of the Republic of Korea in 1948.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Under the agreement dividing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> at the 38th parallel, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xxiv-corps-united-states">US XXIV Corps</a> under Lieutenant General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-r-hodge">John R. Hodge</a> landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incheon">Incheon</a> on 8 September 1945 and accepted the Japanese surrender in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a>. The occupation set up a military government with General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archibald-v-arnold">Archibald Arnold</a> as first governor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lacking knowledge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, the Americans refused to recognise either the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/peoples-republic-of-korea">People's Republic of Korea</a> or the exiled Provisional Government and initially kept Japanese-era officials and police. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> Conference's plan for a trusteeship provoked furious protests, and joint talks with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviets</a> failed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a> shortages, strikes and the Autumn Uprising of 1946 met harsh repression, while right-wing politicians such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syngman-rhee">Syngman Rhee</a> gained ground.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> took the Korean question to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, which supervised elections in the south in May 1948, amid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeju-island">Jeju</a> uprising. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">Republic of Korea</a> was proclaimed on 15 August 1948 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syngman-rhee">Syngman Rhee</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/united-states-army-military-government-in-korea.jpg" alt="United States Army Military Government in Korea" width="320" height="169"><figcaption>Image: Created by jacobolus using Adobe Illustrator. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_States_(1912-1959).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1945 CE – 1948 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Seoul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John R. Hodge, Archibald Arnold, William F. Dean</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Korean, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>South Korea</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>USAMGIK, US military government in Korea</dd></dl></aside></div>
